I agree with almost all of your article, and we are indeed
investigating all the legal ramifications.  However, I'd like to take
issue with the particular paragraph I've quoted.

Our system adminstrators did not 'decide to act as censors'.  They
received from the CoS an allegation of copyright infringement.  They
were not up on the general CoS controversy (and alleged methodology),
and so took this claim at face value, and locked the account while
investigating it *as copyright infringement*, since such infringement
is a violation of Uni policy.

In light of the results of their investigation of the alleged
'copyright infringement', and in view of other evidence at hand,
Martin's account has been reinstated.  It is (IMHO, at least) an
important distinction that the short locking of the account was NOT due
to anything Martin had said.  It was done in the course of a normal
investigation of alleged copyright violation.

(Of course, it might well be that we need to rethink these procedures.
In past, however, alleged copyright violations that we've had to deal
with have revolved around pirate software, and that was the sort of
context in which our admin was thinking.)

