I'm unconnected with this, except that I went to a British University
and had a computer account, but you must remember that students
are given computer accounts so that they can carry out work associated
with their courses.  An account is not a God-given right, it's a
privilege which can probably be withdrawn at any time and which
(rightly IMHO) stays under the control of the appropriate university
computing centre.  The computing centre's first and controlling
priority is not to provide a service to a particular student, but
it is to provide a service to the general university community -
and if safeguarding that service means summarily chopping one
student's account, for whatever reason, so be it.  In this case
they didn't even chop his account - they merely froze it for a short
time and restarted it as soon as they established that the risk
to their first-priority activity was probably very limited.

Of course all this is based on the situation at Edinburgh University
over a decade ago, but I can't imagine that things today at Bristol
are significantly different.

And you must also remember that this happened in Britain, not the USA,
so "freedom of speech" is a significantly smaller factor in any decisions
that the computing centre would've had to make.

