Not sure what 'hoiked' means.  The account was locked for a few hours
(we've found this necessary in past, to preserve evidence) while we
found and talked to Martin.  Having talked to him and determined the
position, the account was unlocked, mere hours later.

>So, it is customary for Uni admin to shoot first and, maybe, think later?

No one has 'shot' anyone.  Quite the contrary, Martin appears to believe
that, all in all, we've been reasonably helpful and supportive.

>Suppose that someone were to allege - falsely - that you were shoving out 
>cracked copies of the latest "warez" through your account. Would the admin
>automata suspend your account also, pending an investigation?

If the allegation appeared plausible and was delivered through formal
legal channels, I would expect so.  (I suspect that anonymous email via
penet wouldn't count, though.)  I would also expect, as was the case
here, that the investigation would be conducted quickly.

>How about if the allegation named a departmental head?  Or the Principal?

I would certainly hope so.  We have in past held them to the same set
of rules and procedures as apply to everyone else.  Insofar as I have
any power personally, I insist upon it.  (And, if we didn't, we'd have
our User's Committee down on us like a ton of bricks.)

>And what makes those second-rate net cops think that they have the right to 
>presume guilt before proof of innocence, thereby inverting the English legal 
>maxim that one is innocent until and unless one is proven guilty by trial 
>before one's peers?  

Sorry, they were not 'presuming guilt'.  They were attempting to
preserve any evidence, in the face of a plausible-sounding accusation,
until they could determine whether there should be any 'charges'.  This
is a common practice in most investigative situations.

At no point have we suggested that we believe that Martin is in any
sense 'guilty'.  He was, however, 'plausibly accused' of an offense
under the law, which does merit certain investigative procedures.
These were conducted swiftly, and in an amicable manner.  One outcome
is that the plausibility of accusations from CoS lawyers will be rated
differently in future.

>I really do think that the Official Visitor to the Uni ought to be told of 
>such monstrous high-handedness.

The University Secretary is up on the issue.  If Martin (or you, or
anyone) wants to report this to the Official Visitor (not sure who that
is, some minor Royal, I expect, sure to have democratic ideals) or to
the Chancellor (Sir Jeremy Morse, I believe), feel free.  Send a letter
to our Vice Chancellor.  Write to the papers as well, if you like; I'd
actually feel more comfortable with a bit more publicity.

Far as I am concerned, and as far as anyone has told me, everyone who
matters is happy with the way we handled it - including the Univeristy
Secretary, our User's Committee (to whom we are responsible in many
ways), and most importantly, Martin himself.  If you've got a problem
with it, then far as I'm concerned, that's your problem.  The people 
we're in business for, our users, seem OK with it.  (Unless, of course,
you *are* one of our users.  In that case, take the pillowcase off of
your head.  You're allowed to be critical of us without needing to hide.)

Alternatively, if you can think of any better way to handle such cases,
keeping in mind that (so far, at least) most such complaints prove to
be justified, and that in the general case the solution has to be
'fair' both to the accused and to the accusor, then I'd love to hear
your ideas.  Just telling me that ours 'aren't good enough' isn't
really very helpful.  Keep in mind that the level of accusation it
has to deal with is not 'imagined violations of poxy little local
rules', but rather allegations that criminal acts have occurred.

