In article <3u6g1n$a7h@netaxs.com>, Rod Keller <rkeller@netaxs.com> wrote:
>At least Paul and Mark made the right decision in the long run. If they'd
>been paying attention to current events on the net and the CoS' history of
>unethical harassment by lawyers, they might have avoided Martin's
>"difficult circumstances" entirely. He seems to have had the greatest 
>difficulty, having his Internet connection suspended for a day.

Actually, only a couple of hours.  Basically only for as long as it
took to find Martin and have a chat.  Also, note that Mark and *that*
Paul were only involved after the fact.  The User's Committee normally
trusts us just to get on with things in a day to day sense.

As to 'paying attentiion...', was a standard 'division of labor'
problem.  I was up on the CoS controversy, but I was not involved
originally in Martin's case since I'm not directly involved in
administration of any of our 'user service' machines. 

Primarily (we all cover a range of things) I am a comms specialist, and
the administrator of some of our net services such as anonymous FTP and
news, and some of our 'non-user' Unixes which run them.  Of course as
news admin I do keep half an eye on the news.admin groups.  (Probably
more than the S/N ratio deserves.)  I first learned of his case about 5
minutes before his account was unlocked.

The admins of our user-service Unixes, on the other hand, take comms,
news, and stuff like that, sort of for-granted.  We supply them, they
use them.  So, they don't watch news.admin.* and weren't up on the CoS
affair.  To the extent that they do use news, they use the groups which
are relevant to what *they* do.

One lesson for us is that it would probably be a good idea for the
relevant person in the comms group to be involved in the initial
response to allegations of misbehaviour if the allegations involve
off-campus networking.  Treating it as a 'user-service' issue is now
probably too simple an approach.

